\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  \textbf  {Resource state shifts earning--spending decisions.} \textbf  {(A) Task paradigm.} The central array of yellow circles represents the monkey's accumulated token holdings (``wallet''), which remained visible during the inter-trial interval (ITI) and operationalized current resource state. Each trial presented one earning and one spending option. Monkeys selected an option by directing their gaze to it and maintaining fixation. During earning, tokens were added to the wallet; during spending, tokens were deducted and water was delivered with each deduction. Monkeys could switch between options by shifting their gaze, and token holdings carried over across trials. \textbf  {(B) Task conditions.} Twelve offer conditions were generated by crossing three earning rates ($V_\text  {Earn}=4, 5$ or $10$ tokens~s$^{-1}$) with four spending reward magnitudes ($V_\text  {Spend}=0, 1, 2$ or $4$ arbitrary units). Each earning outcome added one token, whereas each spending outcome cost one token and delivered the corresponding amount of water. The green-to-red gradient schematically orders conditions from those expected to favor spending to those expected to favor earning; it is illustrative and does not represent a quantitatively computed value ratio. \textbf  {(C) Choice patterns.} Columns show monkeys W, U and T. Earning probability ($P_\text  {Earn}$) is plotted against accumulated tokens for each of the 12 offer conditions. Line colors follow the illustrative offer-condition gradient in B. Only the initial choice on each trial was included. \textbf  {(D) Subjective weights.} Non-parametric weights for the three earning options (red) and four spending options (green) are plotted against accumulated tokens for each monkey. Color shade indicates earning rate or spending reward magnitude, as shown in the key. \textbf  {(E) Psychometric curves.} Earning probability is plotted against the model-derived decision variable $(\Delta V-b)/\gamma $, where $\Delta V=V_E-V_S$ is the difference between the state-dependent earning and spending values, $b$ is the fitted choice bias and $\gamma $ is a positive choice-noise scale. Smaller $\gamma $ corresponds to greater choice sensitivity. Each point represents an offer-condition--resource-state combination; point size is proportional to the number of observations, and color follows the offer-condition map in C. Dashed curves are logistic fits. }}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:behavior}{{1}{3}{\textbf {Resource state shifts earning--spending decisions.} \textbf {(A) Task paradigm.} The central array of yellow circles represents the monkey's accumulated token holdings (``wallet''), which remained visible during the inter-trial interval (ITI) and operationalized current resource state. Each trial presented one earning and one spending option. Monkeys selected an option by directing their gaze to it and maintaining fixation. During earning, tokens were added to the wallet; during spending, tokens were deducted and water was delivered with each deduction. Monkeys could switch between options by shifting their gaze, and token holdings carried over across trials. \textbf {(B) Task conditions.} Twelve offer conditions were generated by crossing three earning rates ($V_\text {Earn}=4, 5$ or $10$ tokens~s$^{-1}$) with four spending reward magnitudes ($V_\text {Spend}=0, 1, 2$ or $4$ arbitrary units). Each earning outcome added one token, whereas each spending outcome cost one token and delivered the corresponding amount of water. The green-to-red gradient schematically orders conditions from those expected to favor spending to those expected to favor earning; it is illustrative and does not represent a quantitatively computed value ratio. \textbf {(C) Choice patterns.} Columns show monkeys W, U and T. Earning probability ($P_\text {Earn}$) is plotted against accumulated tokens for each of the 12 offer conditions. Line colors follow the illustrative offer-condition gradient in B. Only the initial choice on each trial was included. \textbf {(D) Subjective weights.} Non-parametric weights for the three earning options (red) and four spending options (green) are plotted against accumulated tokens for each monkey. Color shade indicates earning rate or spending reward magnitude, as shown in the key. \textbf {(E) Psychometric curves.} Earning probability is plotted against the model-derived decision variable $(\Delta V-b)/\gamma $, where $\Delta V=V_E-V_S$ is the difference between the state-dependent earning and spending values, $b$ is the fitted choice bias and $\gamma $ is a positive choice-noise scale. Smaller $\gamma $ corresponds to greater choice sensitivity. Each point represents an offer-condition--resource-state combination; point size is proportional to the number of observations, and color follows the offer-condition map in C. Dashed curves are logistic fits}{figure.1}{}}
\citation{niederMiller2003compressed,niederDehaene2009number}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Distributed prefrontal tuning represents current resource state.} \textbf  {(A--D) Representative resource-state-selective neurons.} Peristimulus time histograms (mean $\pm $ SEM) aligned to offer onset are shown for an ACC neuron (A) and an OFC neuron (C), together with their mean firing rates in the $-300$ to $300$~ms analysis window (B and D). Resource state was operationalized by accumulated token holdings, which were divided into five approximately logarithmically spaced groups ([1], [2--3], [4--7], [8--15] and [16--24] tokens). Darker-to-lighter shades indicate increasing resource state. The ACC neuron responded most strongly in the highest resource-state group (A,B), whereas the OFC neuron showed maximal firing in the intermediate [8--15]-token group (C,D). \textbf  {(E) Distribution of preferred resource states.} Bars show the percentage of selective neurons preferring each resource-state group in ACC (red), DLPFC (green) and OFC (blue); dark bars show shuffle estimates. \textbf  {(F--H) Population tuning grouped by preferred resource state.} Selective neurons were grouped according to their preferred resource-state group, and their normalized tuning curves were averaged within each group for ACC (F), DLPFC (G) and OFC (H). Shade denotes the preferred group from lowest (dark) to highest (light). \textbf  {(I--K) Tuning re-centered on the preferred resource state.} The curves in F--H were shifted to express resource state relative to each group's preferred level for ACC (I), DLPFC (J) and OFC (K). Error bars denote SEM. Normalized firing rates were higher at relative distances of $\pm 1$ than at $\pm 2$ (ACC, $p=3.31 \times 10^{-41}$; DLPFC, $p=2.50 \times 10^{-36}$; OFC, $p=2.32 \times 10^{-36}$; Wilcoxon rank-sum tests). After resource-state-group labels were shuffled and the complete analysis was repeated, these differences were absent (ACC, $p=0.934$; DLPFC, $p=0.792$; OFC, $p=0.816$; Wilcoxon rank-sum tests; Supplementary Fig.~\ref {fig:supp_resource_tuning}).}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:resource_state_tuning}{{2}{4}{\textbf {Distributed prefrontal tuning represents current resource state.} \textbf {(A--D) Representative resource-state-selective neurons.} Peristimulus time histograms (mean $\pm $ SEM) aligned to offer onset are shown for an ACC neuron (A) and an OFC neuron (C), together with their mean firing rates in the $-300$ to $300$~ms analysis window (B and D). Resource state was operationalized by accumulated token holdings, which were divided into five approximately logarithmically spaced groups ([1], [2--3], [4--7], [8--15] and [16--24] tokens). Darker-to-lighter shades indicate increasing resource state. The ACC neuron responded most strongly in the highest resource-state group (A,B), whereas the OFC neuron showed maximal firing in the intermediate [8--15]-token group (C,D). \textbf {(E) Distribution of preferred resource states.} Bars show the percentage of selective neurons preferring each resource-state group in ACC (red), DLPFC (green) and OFC (blue); dark bars show shuffle estimates. \textbf {(F--H) Population tuning grouped by preferred resource state.} Selective neurons were grouped according to their preferred resource-state group, and their normalized tuning curves were averaged within each group for ACC (F), DLPFC (G) and OFC (H). Shade denotes the preferred group from lowest (dark) to highest (light). \textbf {(I--K) Tuning re-centered on the preferred resource state.} The curves in F--H were shifted to express resource state relative to each group's preferred level for ACC (I), DLPFC (J) and OFC (K). Error bars denote SEM. Normalized firing rates were higher at relative distances of $\pm 1$ than at $\pm 2$ (ACC, $p=3.31 \times 10^{-41}$; DLPFC, $p=2.50 \times 10^{-36}$; OFC, $p=2.32 \times 10^{-36}$; Wilcoxon rank-sum tests). After resource-state-group labels were shuffled and the complete analysis was repeated, these differences were absent (ACC, $p=0.934$; DLPFC, $p=0.792$; OFC, $p=0.816$; Wilcoxon rank-sum tests; Supplementary Fig.~\ref {fig:supp_resource_tuning})}{figure.2}{}}
